# Companion paper. preferred-citation stays the primary result (the measurement);
# this is the case study that argues why the architecture is necessary.
references:
  - type: article
    title: 'A False Confession'
    authors:
      - given-names: Jon
        family-names: DeOliveira
        orcid: 'https://orcid.org/0009-0006-9188-739X'
        affiliation: SOVERYN Intelligence LLC
    doi: 10.5281/zenodo.21650072
    url: 'https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21650072'
    year: 2026
    month: 7
    publisher:
      name: Zenodo
    abstract: >-
      A recorded case in which an agent's audit tooling could not see its own
      actions, and good-faith reasoning produced a fabricated admission of
      fabrication. Argues that an agent has no privileged access to its own past
      behaviour in either direction, and that a self-report of failure cannot be
      scored as ground truth.
  - type: article
    title: 'Scale Does Not Buy Self-Knowledge'
    authors:
      - given-names: Jon
        family-names: DeOliveira
        orcid: 'https://orcid.org/0009-0006-9188-739X'
        affiliation: SOVERYN Intelligence LLC
    doi: 10.5281/zenodo.21712933
    url: 'https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21712933'
    year: 2026
    month: 7
    publisher:
      name: Zenodo
    abstract: >-
      600 trials across five models from 2.2 GB to 118B, asking whether a model
      can report its own recent actions when the evidence channel is incomplete.
      No model ever claimed an action it had not taken (0/150). Models denied
      real actions at 100%, 100%, 100%, 43% and 67%; parameter count explains
      none of the variance. Offered an explicit abstention option, models chose
      it 2 times out of 600.
